{-
   What an overload set refuses - `doc/spec/overloading.md`'s R2 and R4, and the one shape a set
   cannot be resolved at all.

   # resolver: ownership

   `Overload.yana` beside this is the accepting half and is the fixture to read first: it asserts
   that each rule picks the *right* member of the set, by a number. This one asserts that the rules
   never pick by a tiebreak, which is the property R2 exists for - "two candidates that both match
   are an error rather than a contest, because a ranking is what makes overloading unreadable".

   Every message here names the **qualified spellings**, which is R4: the way out of an ambiguity is
   to write which class was meant, so the diagnostic hands the author exactly the text they need
   rather than leaving them to find the classes. `Overload.yana` asserts that the text it hands them
   works.
-}

class Light(a): fn weigh(value: a) -> Int
class Heavy(a): fn weigh(value: a) -> Int

data Box {n: Int}

instance Light(Box): fn weigh(value: Box) -> Int = value.n
instance Heavy(Box): fn weigh(value: Box) -> Int = value.n * 100

-- R2 at a concrete type: both classes have an instance for `Box`, so both candidates are usable and
-- neither is preferred. The two are listed for the author to choose between.
fn concrete() -> Int = weigh(Box {n: 1})

{-
   R3's own example, from the spec: "`fn (a) what(x: a) = size(x)` -- error: ambiguous, name a
   class".

   A different message from the one above and deliberately so. Nothing is *wrong* with either
   candidate here - the argument is a type variable, so both accept it - and what is missing is the
   thing that would decide: an instance to select, or a requirement in scope. So the message names
   the second fix as well, since adding a class to the signature is what R3 says turns this into a
   working program.
-}
fn (a) generic(value: a) -> Int = weigh(value)

{-
   And the one set that cannot be resolved at all, whichever candidate would win.

   The request a position makes is spent *before* selection runs - `resolveValue` takes it, and a
   name read for writing through is a different node from a name read for its value - so it has to
   be something the whole set agrees on. Owning over borrowing is reconcilable and is what `Ord`'s
   `min(->lhs, ->rhs)` beside a plain `min(lhs, rhs)` relies on; a `&` position beside a plain one
   is not, because one needs storage and the other needs a value.
-}
class Adjust(a): fn nudge(&value: a) -> Int

fn nudge(value: Int) -> Int = value

fn conflicted(&box: Int) -> Int = nudge(box)

fn main() -> Int = concrete()
